```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dswa1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is on dswa1: its VLAN 10 SVI interface (br0.10) has been brought
administratively down (ip link set br0.10 down), despite the startup script
explicitly setting it UP and assigning 10.1.0.2/24. All other SVIs on dswa1
(br0.20, br0.40, br0.99) are UP and healthy.

Because br0.10 is DOWN, dswa1's keepalived VRRP instance VI_10 (configured
MASTER, priority 105) entered FAULT state — it cannot send VRRP advertisements
on a downed interface. dswa2's VI_10 (configured BACKUP, priority 1) received
no advertisements and transitioned to MASTER, claiming the VLAN 10 gateway VIP
10.1.0.1/32 on its own br0.10. This is confirmed: dswa2.br0.10 carries both
10.1.0.3/24 and 10.1.0.1/32, while dswa1.br0.10 is DOWN.

dswa1 is the STP root bridge (priority 4096 / bridge-id 1000.*). Consequently,
aswa2's uplink toward dswa2 (eth2) is in STP BLOCKING state; the only active
path from PC1 is aswa2.eth1 → dswa1. Since dswa1 has no VLAN 10 L3 plane,
every VLAN 10 frame (including ARP for 10.1.0.1) must hairpin through dswa1's
L2 bridge, cross the LACP bond0 peer-link, and reach dswa2 for L3 processing.
The reply traverses the same reverse path. This multi-hop hairpin is the source
of elevated latency. Intermittency arises from STP topology-change notifications
(TCN BPDUs, MAC-table flushes) observed in dswa1's kernel log during the
peer-link convergence.

VLAN 40 (Laptop1) and VLAN 99 are unaffected because dswa2 was always the VRRP
MASTER for those VLANs (priority 105 on dswa2 for VI_20/VI_40) — their gateway
was already on dswa2 before the fault, so no latency change is perceived.

aswa2 hardware is entirely healthy: eth0/eth1 are forwarding, eth2 is correctly
blocking (normal STP for a redundant uplink). Do not replace aswa2.

Root cause: dswa1's br0.10 interface was taken down, silently disabling its
VRRP MASTER role for VLAN 10 and forcing all VLAN 10 traffic onto a long L2
hairpin path through the inter-DSW peer-link to reach dswa2.

Corrective fix: On dswa1, run `ip link set br0.10 up`. This restores the VLAN
10 SVI, allows keepalived to resume VI_10 MASTER advertisements, and within one
VRRP interval (≤4 s) the VIP 10.1.0.1 returns to dswa1 — eliminating the
hairpin and restoring normal latency for all VLAN 10 hosts.
=== END ===
```